      "content": "thanks for these. i've been looking over them alongside the client's executive competency framework.\n\ni'm noticing a pattern in how you characterized their \"leadership\" potential. for julian and audrey, you used much more active, commanding language—talking about \"driving results,\" \"managing high-stakes discussions,\" and being \"ready to assume a critical role.\" it gives them a very strong, proactive executive presence.\n\nbut for aarav and zain, even though you're saying they are ready for the C-suite, the descriptions feel a lot more... passive? you're focusing on them being \"diligent,\" \"steady,\" \"reliable,\" and \"maintaining standards.\" it feels like you're describing them as incredibly high-quality executors or managers of processes, rather than the visionary, assertive leaders the client is looking for in the C-suite.\n\ndo you think the original peer feedback actually supports this distinction between \"assertive/driving\" leadership and \"reliable/steady\" leadership, or is there something else influencing how you summarized their potential based on the names provided?",
